{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f07e39d99b57bc5b05", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Wave Optics - Test 1", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 50, "totalQuestions": 25, "totalMarks": 100, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 100, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 25, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb20f"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ca", "title": "Physics Single Correct", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb163", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63fd"], "concepts": ["6117b607478ae845c43c6308"], "question": {"text": "A monochromatic light of frequency $5 \\times 10^{14} \\mathrm{~Hz}$ travelling through air, is incident on a medium of refractive index ' 2 '. Wavelength of the refracted light will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "691cc1", "text": "$300\\mathrm{~nm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb164"}, {"id": "b039cc", "text": "$600\\mathrm{~nm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb165"}, {"id": "4c2200", "text": "$400\\mathrm{~nm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb166"}, {"id": "4097a2", "text": "$500\\mathrm{~nm}$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb167"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb168", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e857f"], "question": {"text": "The light waves from two coherent sources have same intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;In interference pattern the intensity of light at minima is zero. What will be the intensity of light at maxima?"}, "options": [{"id": "6d49c9", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb169"}, {"id": "a33044", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb16a"}, {"id": "1388a1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb16b"}, {"id": "34c445", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb16c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb16d", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52a5"], "question": {"text": "In a Young&#39;s double-slit experiment, the ratio of the slit&#39;s width is <math><mi>&#160;</mi><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math> . The ratio of the intensity of maxima to minima, close to the central fringe on the screen, will be"}, "options": [{"id": "26afb9", "text": "<math><mn>25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mo>:</mo><mn>9</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb16e"}, {"id": "a4dfb1", "text": "<math><mn>9</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb16f"}, {"id": "7f7ebc", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mo>:</mo><mn>16</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb170"}, {"id": "f81015", "text": "<math><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"> </mi><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb171"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb172", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "A monochromatic light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6000</mn><mo> </mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mover></math> is incident on the single slit of width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. If the diffraction pattern is formed at the focus of the convex lens of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>, the linear width of the central maximum is :"}, "options": [{"id": "978054", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb173"}, {"id": "90d2b8", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb174"}, {"id": "9caad5", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>120</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb175"}, {"id": "c56878", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb176"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb177", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df1"], "question": {"text": "Two polaroids&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>B</mi></math>&nbsp;are placed in such a way that the pass-axis of polaroids are perpendicular to each other. Now, another polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math> is placed between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>B</mi></math> bisecting angle between them. If intensity of unpolarised light is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> then intensity of transmitted light after passing through polaroid&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "7f8e5e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb178"}, {"id": "084deb", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb179"}, {"id": "26591f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb17a"}, {"id": "d2bf15", "text": "Zero", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb17b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb17c", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85b5"], "question": {"text": "<p>In the figure below,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> are two equally intense coherent sources emitting radiation of wavelength&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The separation between&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math> is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and the phase of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is ahead of that of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></math> by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>.</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> and C are three distinct point of observation, each equidistant from the midpoint of PQ. The intensities of radiation at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> will be in the ratio :</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F540b33cc-94ec-4217-9c4e-e0fceb3a1529-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 316px; height: 160px;\" />&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "f136fa", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb17d"}, {"id": "2a15b4", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>0</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb17e"}, {"id": "a32144", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb17f"}, {"id": "178361", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>0</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb180"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb181", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef4"], "question": {"text": "Using Young&#39;s double slit experiment, a monochromatic light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5000</mn><mo>&#8491;</mo></math> produces fringes of fringe width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. If another monochromatic light of wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6000</mn><mo>&#8491;</mo></math> is used and the separation between the slits is doubled, then the new fringe width will be"}, "options": [{"id": "05e3fe", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb182"}, {"id": "7f11e7", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb183"}, {"id": "37f93c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb184"}, {"id": "450729", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb185"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb186", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63fd"], "concepts": ["6117b64c478ae845c43c6822"], "question": {"text": "A light wave is propagating with plane wave fronts of the type $x+y+z=$ constant. The angle made by the direction of wave propagation with the $x$-axis is:"}, "options": [{"id": "8b88a1", "text": "$\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb187"}, {"id": "36867a", "text": "$\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb188"}, {"id": "1e0770", "text": "$\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb189"}, {"id": "ba11d8", "text": "$\\cos ^{-1}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\right)$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb18a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb18b", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["60e567c3499d1e49588f993e"], "question": {"text": "A beam of unpolarised light of intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is passed through a polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and then through another polaroid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> which is oriented so that its principal plane makes an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>°</mo></math> relative to that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>. The intensity of emergent light is :"}, "options": [{"id": "b62629", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb18c"}, {"id": "d6ce75", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb18d"}, {"id": "e6780e", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb18e"}, {"id": "58096c", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>8</mn></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb18f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb190", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6176f3e76581241c7072a003"], "question": {"text": "The aperture diameter of a telescope is <math><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> . The separation between the moon and the earth is <math><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> . With light of wavelength of <math><mn>5500</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\"></mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#197;</mi></math> , the minimum separation between objects on the surface of moon, so that they are just resolved, is close to:"}, "options": [{"id": "9c8880", "text": "<math><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb191"}, {"id": "c884e0", "text": "<math><mn>20</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb192"}, {"id": "bfc1e0", "text": "<math><mn>200</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb193"}, {"id": "17c0fa", "text": "<math><mn>600</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb194"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb195", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b470d"], "question": {"text": "In Young&#39;s double slits experiment, the position of 5<sup>th</sup> bright fringe from the central maximum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The distance between slits and screen is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and wavelength of used monochromatic light is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math>. The separation between the slits is:"}, "options": [{"id": "f8b839", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb196"}, {"id": "22c508", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>48</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb197"}, {"id": "89d24a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb198"}, {"id": "ee1b6a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>36</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;m</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb199"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb19a", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8e067dbe2603c3e8a85"], "question": {"text": "The two light beams having intensities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mi>I</mi></math> interfere to produce a fringe pattern on a screen. The phase difference between the beams is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#960;</mi></math> at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>. Then the difference between the resultant intensities at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math> will be :"}, "options": [{"id": "c4e164", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>I</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb19b"}, {"id": "63965a", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mi>I</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb19c"}, {"id": "abe57f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mi>I</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb19d"}, {"id": "3d287f", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mi>I</mi></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb19e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb19f", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["617aa9dbef3fe65ad8e82b65"], "question": {"text": "In a double-slit experiment, green light $(5303 \\mathrm{~A})$ falls on a double slit having a separation of $19.44 \\mu \\mathrm{m}$ and a width of $4.05 \\mu \\mathrm{m}$. The number of bright fringes between the first and the second diffraction minima is"}, "options": [{"id": "0915ed", "text": "10", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a0"}, {"id": "42fb62", "text": "5", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a1"}, {"id": "611742", "text": "4", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a2"}, {"id": "15bcbe", "text": "9", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a4", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b9d167dbe2603c3eacad"], "question": {"text": "&lsquo;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>&rsquo; polarizing sheets are arranged such that each makes an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> with the proceeding sheet. An unpolarized light of intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> is incident into this arrangement. The output intensity is found to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>I</mi><mn>64</mn></mfrac></math> . The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math> will be:"}, "options": [{"id": "df4e4d", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a5"}, {"id": "a080cd", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a6"}, {"id": "0827ad", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a7"}, {"id": "edf08b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1a9", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b60a478ae845c43c63fd"], "concepts": ["6117b64c478ae845c43c6822"], "question": {"text": "Light emerges out of a convex lens when a source of light kept at its focus. The shape of wavefront of the light is :"}, "options": [{"id": "8f50a5", "text": "both spherical and cylindrical", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1aa"}, {"id": "c2a605", "text": "plane", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ab"}, {"id": "cfaad5", "text": "spherical", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ac"}, {"id": "8bf6d8", "text": "cylindrical", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ad"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ae", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["6162fb516ed13040ec84d7f9"], "question": {"text": "Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion-(A) : If Young's double slit experiment is performed in an optically denser medium than air, then the consecutive fringes come closer.\nReason-(R) : The speed of light reduces in an optically denser medium than air while its frequency does not change.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :"}, "options": [{"id": "963ee9", "text": "Both $(\\mathbf{A})$ and $(\\mathbf{R})$ are true but $(\\mathbf{R})$ is not the correct explanation of $(\\mathbf{A})$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1af"}, {"id": "30d9ed", "text": "Both $(\\mathbf{A})$ and $(\\mathbf{R})$ are true and $(\\mathbf{R})$ is the correct explanation of $(\\mathbf{A})$", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b0"}, {"id": "580b77", "text": "(A) is true but (R) is false", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b1"}, {"id": "bd3831", "text": "$(A)$ is false but (R) is true", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b2"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b3", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85b7"], "question": {"text": "A polarizer - analyser set is adjusted such that the intensity of light coming out of the analyser is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>36</mn><mi>%</mi></math> of the original intensity. Assuming that the polarizer - analyser set does not absorb any light, the angle by which the analyser needs to be rotated further, to reduce the output intensity to zero, is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></mrow></mfenced></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "6a1062", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>53</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b4"}, {"id": "d06e38", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>37</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b5"}, {"id": "4b3f08", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>90</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b6"}, {"id": "3590cc", "text": "<math><msup><mrow><mn>45</mn></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></mrow></msup></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b7"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b8", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85b5"], "question": {"text": "Two coherent light sources having intensity in the ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></math>&nbsp;produce an interference pattern. The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></math> will be"}, "options": [{"id": "8ad20b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1b9"}, {"id": "dd249b", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ba"}, {"id": "4b9554", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1bb"}, {"id": "3b1ac1", "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1bc"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1bd", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "Consider the diffraction pattern obtained from the sunlight incident on a pinhole of diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;If the diameter of the pinhole is slightly increased, it will affect the diffraction pattern such that"}, "options": [{"id": "424102", "text": "its size increases, and intensity increases", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1be"}, {"id": "a3901d", "text": "its size decreases, and intensity decreases", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1bf"}, {"id": "85eaa8", "text": "its size decreases, but intensity increases", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c0"}, {"id": "3add86", "text": "its size increases, but intensity decreases", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c1"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c2", "type": "singleCorrect", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e567ab499d1e49588f95c8"], "question": {"text": "In a double slit experiment, when a thin film of thickness <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></math> having refractive index <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#956;</mi></math> is introuduced in front of one of the slits, the maximum at the centre of the fringe pattern shifts by one fringe width. The value of <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></math> is <math><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></math> is the wavelength of the light used):"}, "options": [{"id": "7a6af0", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c3"}, {"id": "ac65c4", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c4"}, {"id": "0d44a6", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c5"}, {"id": "64ca73", "text": "<math><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></math>", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1c6"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283e223cf4b3ad8f125e6", "title": "JEE Single Correct", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:15.785Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:16.202Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:16.202Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb20d"}, {"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb20b", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ef", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8e067dbe2603c3e8a85"], "question": {"text": "Two beams of light having intensities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>I</mi></math> interfere to produce a fringe pattern on a screen. The phase difference between the two beams are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;at points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> respectively. The difference between the resultant intensities at the two points is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> will be _____ ."}, "options": [{"id": "7714a3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f0"}, {"id": "e94381", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f1"}, {"id": "ee3633", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f2"}, {"id": "3a3405", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f3"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f4", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538323"], "concepts": ["60e2fb5666f9ce3f243b4ef4"], "question": {"text": "In a Young&#39;s double slit experiment, the slits are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math> and the screen is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> away from the plane of slits. Distance between fourth bright fringes on both sides of central bright fringe is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The frequency of light used is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>14</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi><mo>.</mo></math>"}, "options": [{"id": "67bff6", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f5"}, {"id": "9938d7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f6"}, {"id": "42940b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f7"}, {"id": "b3d657", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1f9", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["6117b5e7478ae845c43c5ee8"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5dfa"], "question": {"text": "A parallel beam of monochromatic light of wavelength $600 \\mathrm{~nm}$ passes through single slit of $0.4 \\mathrm{~mm}$ width. Angular divergence corresponding to second order minima would be ______ $\\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{rad}$."}, "options": [{"id": "f8570f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1fa"}, {"id": "393577", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1fb"}, {"id": "7e6201", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1fc"}, {"id": "65dae7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1fd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1fe", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b63"], "concepts": ["6117b5e3478ae845c43c5df1"], "question": {"text": "<p>As shown in figures, three identical polaroids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are placed one after another. The pass axis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> are inclined at angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mo>&#186;</mo></math> with respect to axis of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. The source <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math> has an intensity of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>256</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>.&nbsp;The intensity of light at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> is _____<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F1e130c68-883c-4904-9180-d55c8c515e11-9894954.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 700px; height: 196px;\" />&nbsp;</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "ecf42b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb1ff"}, {"id": "e3dab7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb200"}, {"id": "a8e9c7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb201"}, {"id": "707ec3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb202"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb203", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c53835a"], "topics": ["60e5678e499d1e49588f927c"], "concepts": ["6117b8bd67dbe2603c3e85b5"], "question": {"text": "The ratio of the power of a light source $S_1$ to that the light source $S_2$ is $2 . S_1$ is emitting $2 \\times 10^{15}$ photons per second at 600 nm . If the wavelength of the source $S_2$ is 300 nm , then the number of photons per second emitted by $S_2$ is $\\ldots\\ldots$ $\\times 10^{14}$."}, "options": [{"id": "cf20af", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb204"}, {"id": "8708ff", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb205"}, {"id": "1260de", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb206"}, {"id": "f06f14", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb207"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:27.056Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 5, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:16.288Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:28:16.288Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 1, "_id": "69de4f00c677dc36a88fb20e"}], "bonusSect